# Uncle Bob Quality Gates

Canon: `memory-bank/constitution/uncle-bob-quality-gates.md`  
Sources: unclebob/swarm-forge, Acceptance-Pipeline-Specification (adapted for JS/TS).

## Before marking any coding task done

Run and pass (or run `npm run quality`):

1. `npm run typecheck`
2. `npm test`
3. For **Tier A** domain changes (`packages/shared` calc/progression/journal metrics, related tests): also `npm run test:acceptance`, `npm run test:crap`, and `npm run test:mutate` (or `npm run quality`)
4. Prefer `npm run quality` / `quality:quick` over claiming done by eyeballing code
5. Do **not** claim done if any gate fails — fix root cause (no blind retries)

Note: full-repo `npm run lint` may still fail on legacy UI; the Uncle Bob gate uses calc-focused eslint + crap-check until lint debt is cleared.

## Spec vs code

- Prefer expressing new Tier A behavior in `features/**/*.feature` (Gherkin) first when adding user-visible rules.
- Unit tests under `tests/` remain required; acceptance is not a substitute.
- Humans review Gherkin; do not skip gates by “manual code looks fine”.

## Complexity

- Keep new/changed pure functions under eslint complexity limit (pilot ≤ 12).
- If a file grows past ~100 mutation sites, split modules before expanding further.

## Do not

- Install SwarmForge tmux swarm for routine fixes
- Hand-edit mutation manifests
- Lower thresholds silently — change constitution + this rule together
- Skip iPhone 390–430px verify for UI layout changes (existing project rule)
